I. INTRODUCTION
Accurate computation of rotor loads has always been crucial in vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) vehicle flight studies. Prediction of rotor loads relating to different rotor configurations and operational conditions allows for precise engineering analysis and optimization in the designing phase of rotor development. This understanding enables optimized rotor design by tailoring blade shapes, sizes, and materials to enhance performance and efficiency while minimizing structural loads which is of great importance. Furthermore, rotor performance models are an integral part of a flight dynamic model which is necessary to predict system level performance of rotary wing aircraft. In the past decades, different methods were exploited to predict the rotor load. Not surprisingly, three-dimensional computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations are among the highest-fidelity approaches applied for such
cases.1–3 Despite its high accuracy and precision, CFD models are computationally expensive and time-consuming that limits its usage in flight dynamic models and optimization framework. On the other
hand, there have been medium and lower fidelity modeling approaches that expedite the solving process at the expense of some accuracy. The blade element momentum theory (BEMT) is one such medium-fidelity modeling methodology to predict rotor loads.4 BEMT offers a good compromise between accuracy and computational efficiency, providing reasonably accurate rotor load predictions while being significantly faster than CFD models. Its flexibility allows for the inclusion of bladespecific design features, making it useful for preliminary design and performance assessments. Recently, Cornelius et al. introduced a hybrid BEMT-URANS CFD methodology to accurately predict rotor performance for both single and coaxial rotor configurations, achieving significant reductions in computational time compared to traditional CFD methods. This approach utilizes a high-resolution BEMT rotor model and GPU-accelerated simulations to perform highly parallelized computations, resulting in several orders of magnitude lower computational cost while maintaining high fidelity in the modeling of UAM flight conditions. The methodology demonstrated thrust and torque predictions
