within 4%–5% of experimental data obtained from NASA’s Langley Subsonic Tunnel Facility, highlighting its accuracy and efficiency.5 However, BEMT models, though faster, are still not fast enough for real-time simulations or iterative optimization processes. Moreover, significant advancements in aerial vehicle technologies over recent years have highlighted the critical need to enhance the computational power and sophistication of predictive modeling methods. These breakthroughs include developments in unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) vehicles, all of which demand more efficient lower order models to support their design, analysis, and operational optimization. Addressing these challenges requires leveraging much faster surrogate models to accurately simulate and predict the complex aerodynamic behaviors and performance characteristics of modern aerial vehicles. Peters et al. investigated the application of a proper orthogonal decomposition (POD)-based reduced-order model (ROM) to predict rotor distributed loads. By leveraging computational fluid dynamics (CFD) results, the POD ROM efficiently predicts distributed pressures on rotor blades subjected to variations in twist and taper ratios, showcasing substantial computational cost reduction compared to traditional CFD simulations, making it suitable for rotor design optimization in both hovering and forward flight scenarios.6 Today, thanks to data-driven methods, there exist alternative predictive approaches that bypass previous classical calculations. The stateof-the-art machine learning (ML) methods have been being used for several years in many mechanical and aerospace applications such as fluid dynamics, aerodynamics, aeroacoustics, combustion, and structural research.7 Machine learning methods may be exploited as black boxes that are completely agnostic to the physics of the problem, or they may appear as physics-integrated structures that can evaluate the accuracy of the found patterns of the data based on the provided physical knowledge. Makkar et al. developed a machine learning method to efficiently correct a low-fidelity trim analysis model of a compound helicopter using sparse high-fidelity data. By iteratively selecting data where errors exceed tolerance, the approach reduces the number of required data points by 79%, while maintaining accuracy within a 5% tolerance in key regions. Testing shows 95% 98% of predictions meet error tolerance goals, outperforming purely data-driven models.8 Recently, Rao et al. developed a framework to train machine learning-based surrogate models for predicting the endurance and range of eVTOL vehicles, using data from a large number of simulated designs. The surrogate models, including decision trees, K-nearest neighbors, linear regression, and neural networks, accurately mimic complex physics-based models with a mean average percentage error of less than 2%, while significantly reducing computational demands.9 Employing data-driven models offers distinct advantages. They leverage historical data and computational techniques to establish simplified relationships between inputs and outputs. Once trained, these models can rapidly predict rotor performance with minimal computational effort, facilitating quick design iterations and scalability for parametric analyses. These approaches reduce the computational burden associated with detailed aerodynamic computations, making them suitable for efficient rotorcraft design and optimization. The present work introduces a novel approach using neural network (NN) and blade element theory (BET) to predict three-dimensional hub forces and moments of a rotor. This work presents a fast and computationally efficient machine learning-based model for predicting rotor
performance across various configurations and operational conditions. The approach utilizes an innovative surrogate model, termed NNBET, which integrates an artificial neural network with a blade element model. NN-BET achieves a computational speedup of an order of magnitude compared to the BEMT model, one of the fastest available physics-based models. Additionally, the incorporation of physicsbased principles into the NN-BET framework significantly enhances its accuracy relative to traditional neural network-based surrogate models.
II. METHODOLOGY
This section discusses the architecture of the NN-BET model, data structure developed for training, and the training process in detail.
A. Architecture of ML-based model
This section discusses the architecture of the developed NN-BET model, where a feed-forward neural network is used to compute induced inflow across rotor disk and this information is being leveraged by a blade element model to compute blade loads. This architecture is somewhat similar to a traditional BEMT model where inflow is computed using momentum theory and blade loads are computed using a BET model. However, BEMT involves an iterative process to solve nonlinear systems of equations since both momentum theory and blade element equations are coupled to each other. This iterative process makes the whole BEMT process time-consuming. The NNBET model improves upon this traditional BEMT approach by removing this iterative solutions of inflow and blade loads. Instead, NN-BET leverages the predictive capabilities of a neural network to compute inflow from rotor configuration and operating condition which is directly used by BET to compute blade loads. Figure 1 illustrates the differences between NN-BET and BEMT approaches.
