B. Data structure
To train the neural networks of the NN-BET and the NN model, a data structure is generated that consists of 184 unique airfoils with 100 random samples generated for each airfoil. These samples span 13 operational and geometric features, discussed earlier (Table I). While generating the dataset, reasonable ranges of these features are considered, which is presented in Table III. The ranges are carefully defined to encompass diverse yet practical configurations. It should be noted that the rotor’s radius and the angular velocity are fixed for all sample cases. In Table III, index i 1⁄4 0; 1; ...; 200 indicates the point’s number on the surface of the airfoil. The x and y coordinates are consistent across all samples for a given airfoil, allowing each airfoil to be analyzed under distinct operational scenarios. As a result, the dataset contains a total of 18 400 samples. To create this extensive dataset, a BEMT-based model is utilized, since it is relatively computationally inexpensive compared to other higher-fidelity CFD models. This BEMT model4 incorporates a dynamic inflow distribution over the rotor disk, which is defined by Eqs. (5)–(7). The mean inflow, denoted by k0 in Eq. (5), is obtained using a momentum theory-based approach while solving in an iterative manner. The dynamic inflow enables BEMT to analyze rotors in
