III. RESULTS
The results derived from evaluating the trained models are detailed in this section. Both NN and NN-BET models were trained on a dataset comprising 16 800 distinct scenarios involving 168
different airfoils. For validation, the models were tested on 1600 unseen cases generated from 16 airfoils that were excluded from the training dataset, ensuring an unbiased assessment of their performance. Consequently, this validation represents the first encounter of the NN and NN-BET models with this distinct test dataset. The scalability and generalizability of the NN-based models are showcased, utilizing the hyperparameters outlined in the Table IV. Figure 3 illustrates the predictive performance of the developed models in estimating non-dimensional rotor loads. Predictions from the NN-BET and NN models are plotted on the y-axis, while the reference values, derived from BEMT, are plotted on the x-axis. The 45 line represents perfect agreement between predicted and reference values, indicating zero error. Data points closer to this line indicate higher predictive accuracy. The results show that the NN-BET model achieves superior precision compared to the standalone NN model, particularly for the thrust component (Fz), where most predictions closely align with the reference values, as evidenced by the clustering of data points along the bisector line. The prediction accuracy of the results in Fig. 3 is quantified using the coefficient of determination (R2) for each load type across all test set samples. Figure 4 presents the R2 values for forces and moments predicted by the NN and NN-BET models. The NN-BET model consistently achieves significantly higher R2 values across various load types compared to the NN model. Notably, Fig. 4 demonstrates the high accuracy of the NN-BET model in predicting Fz, with an R2 value approaching 1. This indicates the model’s ability to estimate thrust with minimal error, underscoring its reliability in accurately capturing thrust forces. The prediction accuracy is further evaluated by analyzing the distribution of absolute error values for the two ML-based models in predicting rotor loads. The box chart of Fig. 5 indicates that the NN model exhibits substantially higher variability than the NN-BET model. The medians and quartiles, depicted by the box plots, indicate that the NN-BET model generally achieves lower median error values and exhibits reduced variability compared to the NN model. Rotor aeromechanics is a complicated phenomenon characterized by complex blade-vortex interactions, non-uniform inflow which leads to the complex dependence of the loads on the input parameters, and thus accurately capturing the underlying input-output relationship is challenging. Traditional NN models directly map input features to loads, making them more prone to errors and reduced performance. In contrast, the NN-BET model employs a hybrid approach, using a neural network to predict an intermediate parameter—the mean inflow—which is processed through a BET framework to compute the loads. This integration of physics-based principles significantly improves prediction accuracy and reliability of the NN-BET model under diverse conditions. The above observations clearly demonstrate that the NN-BET model offers significantly higher performance compared to the traditional NN model. Table V summarizes the runtime of each modeling approach to process a single sample, obtained using an Intel(R) Core(TM) i913900 CPU with 32 GB of RAM and an Intel(R) UHD Graphics 770 GPU. The reported elapsed times for each approach are computed as the average over 1600 test samples, ensuring a consistent and unbiased evaluation across methods. Both ML-based models demonstrate significantly faster computational performance than the traditional BEMT. In particular, the NN-BET model is an order of magnitude faster than
