IV. CONCLUSION
This study focuses on developing computationally efficient models that can accurately predict forces and moments generated by a rotor. Toward this end, a novel hybrid machine learning model, NNBET, is introduced that combines neural networks with blade element
theory (BET) principles to predict rotor performance with enhanced accuracy, reliability, and efficiency, compared to the physics-based models and the traditional NN model. The key insights of the study are given as follows:
• The NN-BET model is shown to be an order of magnitude faster than the traditional BEMT method (one of the fastest physicsbased models) while maintaining comparable accuracy and precision. • The incorporation of physics-based principles in the NN-BET model yields significantly higher accuracy compared to the traditional neural network (NN) model, as evidenced by the R2 values shown in Fig. 4.
The improved accuracy and time efficiency of NN-BET make it a suitable candidate for optimization and design applications, offering a practical solution for real-time or large-scale rotor performance predictions, as well as multidisciplinary frameworks that require efficient modeling of rotor dynamics. Regardless of the selected framework or application, the current version of NN-BET provides highly efficient and reliable rotor load estimations, achieving computational speeds an order of magnitude faster than physics-based models such as (BEMT), one of the fastest conventional approaches. Furthermore, the NN-BET
